Thanks; agreed. The revision is posted as "[PATCH v2] dotfiles/notmuch:
Spec reading and sending through lab-mail" [1]. It drops the phases
and the ordering, and states only the end result in two sections,
"beastie" and "emacs". The phase 3 clean up became an end-state entry
saying that Emacs does not refer to the jj maildir. The dependency on
the agent's workflow is now an assumption under out of scope, beside
a new entry leaving libexec/mail/ and the default profile to the
agent's own spec. The commit message no longer mentions phases
either. The patch carries an interdiff against v1.

For the record, here is how I intend to organize the work, which the
commits will show:

1. support: the ports, the pat@p7x.dev allowed sender in lab-mail,
   the lists profile, ~/.mbsyncrc and ~/.msmtprc, all beside the jj
   maildir client and changing nothing it uses.
2. switch: Emacs reads the lists profile and sends through lab-mail.
3. clean up: remove what only the jj maildir client used on beastie.

Step 2 waits until the agent's process-email workflow reads and sends
through lab-mail too. Once Emacs sends over SMTP, your mail stops
reaching the jj maildir, so an agent that reads only the jj maildir
stops seeing it. Moving process-email needs its own spec; step 1 does
not depend on it.

